FreeBSD-claude-native
=====================

Installs Anthropic's Claude Code as a native FreeBSD executable, with no
Linux compatibility layer. The installer downloads the official Linux build
of Claude Code and the matching official FreeBSD build of Bun, verifies both
against their published SHA-256 sums, and converts Claude Code to run on
FreeBSD Bun. Tested on FreeBSD 15.1, amd64.


Install
-------

Run these as your own user, not as root:

    tar -xzf FreeBSD-claude-native.tar.gz -C /tmp
    sh /tmp/FreeBSD-claude-native/install.sh
    rm -rf /tmp/FreeBSD-claude-native

The installer keeps everything it needs under ~/.local/share/claude/, so
the extracted files can be deleted afterward, as above.

By default the latest release is installed. To choose one:

    sh /tmp/FreeBSD-claude-native/install.sh stable     # stable channel
    sh /tmp/FreeBSD-claude-native/install.sh 2.1.319    # a specific version

Claude Code needs four packages: ripgrep, ugrep, bfs and bash. Any that are
missing are installed with pkg, through doas, sudo or su, so run the
installer from a terminal.

At the end, the installer asks whether you will use Claude Code in a
terminal on a graphical desktop. If you answer yes, it also installs
whichever of these are missing: symbol fonts (noto-sans-symbols,
noto-sans-symbols2) and a clipboard tool for pasting images (xclip, or
wl-clipboard on Wayland).

Claude Code is installed to ~/.local/bin/claude. If that directory is not
on your PATH, the installer shows how to add it. Its files go in
~/.local/share/claude/, or $XDG_DATA_HOME/claude/ if you set XDG_DATA_HOME.


Update
------

Updates install themselves. Each time you start claude (at most every 8
hours), a background check looks for a newer version of your release
channel (Claude Code's autoUpdatesChannel setting: latest, the default, or
stable) and converts and installs it. Your next session says it was
updated, or that the update failed; the details are in
~/.local/share/claude/freebsd/update.log. A failed update leaves the
installed version in place.

These updates use the installer's own copy. To update right away:

    sh ~/.local/share/claude/freebsd/install.sh

Claude Code's own updater stays turned off, because it would install the
Linux build. To install a new release of this tarball, extract it and run
its install.sh as above; that also refreshes the installer's copy.


Contents
--------

    install.sh     the installer (POSIX sh, base system tools only)
    regraft.js     converts the Linux build to run on FreeBSD Bun
    bun-ant/       JavaScript replacements for parts of Anthropic's runtime
    LICENSE        MIT license for the files in this tarball
    README.install this file


License
-------

The files in this tarball are MIT licensed; see LICENSE. Parts of bun-ant/
